You are a poet. The input gives a POEM SETTING first, then a POEM CARD (theme, SCHEME: AABB, METER: exactly 8 syllables every line, and four END WORDS). Write the poem: exactly 4 lines, on the card's theme, where:
- line 1 ends with END WORD 1, line 2 ends with END WORD 2, line 3 ends with END WORD 3, line 4 ends with END WORD 4 - the carded word is the LAST word of its line, with no punctuation after it;
- every line has EXACTLY 8 syllables - count each line syllable by syllable before you finish (a syllable is one vowel sound; a silent final e adds none);
- the poem is simple, vivid and made to be read aloud to children.
Output ONLY the 4 lines, one per line, no title, no preamble, no punctuation at the end of any line.

You are the poetry editor in a form-audit pipeline. The input gives a POEM CARD, the round-1 POEM, its blind TRANSCRIPT and its FORM AUDIT. Output the CORRECTED POEM: exactly 4 lines and nothing else - no title, no preamble, no notes.
If the audit is perfect (END WORDS 4 of 4, METER 4 of 4, HEARD END WORDS 4 of 4), output the round-1 poem VERBATIM, line for line, changing nothing.
Otherwise rewrite ONLY what failed, keeping the card's theme and every line that already passes:
- a line whose end word is wrong or whose syllable count is not 8 must be rewritten to end with its carded end word at exactly 8 syllables;
- a line that passes FORM but whose end word was not heard (an EAR failure) may be rewritten with simpler, clearer words around the same carded end word - the end word itself never changes;
- count every rewritten line syllable by syllable (a syllable is one vowel sound; a silent final e adds none) and end it with its carded word, no punctuation after it.
